{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     ads.hbs — ads.txt, from a custom setting. Plain text: no {{!< default}},
     or the layout wraps the records in HTML and every verifier reads zero
     authorised sellers.

     ── WHAT ADS.TXT IS FOR ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
     IAB Tech Lab's Authorized Digital Sellers spec: a text file at /ads.txt
     listing, one record per line, every party allowed to sell or resell this
     site's ad inventory — so a programmatic buyer can check that whoever is
     offering "namastesalesforce.com" inventory actually has the right to.
     Its absence does not hurt a site; a WRONG one does, because buyers drop
     inventory that fails verification. app-ads.txt is the same spec for
     apps, keyed off the app store listing — same format, different path, so
     this one template can back both routes if that day comes.

     And honestly: this site sells no programmatic inventory today, so it
     does not need an ads.txt, and serving none is the correct state. The
     template exists so that the day a network is signed, the publisher
     pastes the network's lines into Ghost Admin -> Design -> theme settings
     and is done — no theme release, no redeploy, no developer in the loop.
     That is the failure this file exists to avoid: seller records hardcoded
     into a template, going stale behind a deploy nobody schedules.

     ── WHY THE TRIPLE-STASH ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Handlebars' double stash escapes for HTML — an & in a pasted record
     would ship as &amp;, which is a literal corruption in a plain-text
     file that ad systems parse byte-for-byte. The setting is emitted raw in
     a triple stash. Safe here because the output is text/plain, never
     rendered as HTML; nothing can be injected into a page that is not one.

     When the setting is empty the file is a single # comment — comments are
     valid per the spec, and an effectively-empty ads.txt means "nobody is
     authorised", which is true.

     ── HOW THIS IS REACHED (verified against Ghost 6.57.1) ─────────────────
     Ghost has no built-in /ads.txt handling, so unlike robots.txt and
     llms.txt a routes.yaml route CAN own this — but only the trailing-slash
     form. A bare GET /ads.txt has a file extension, so static-theme's
     express.static answers it with fallthrough disabled and 404s before the
     SiteRouter ever runs; GET /ads.txt/ has no extension, passes through,
     and reaches the route. The proxy must therefore rewrite /ads.txt to
     /ads.txt/ (the crawlers request the bare path). Details in routes.yaml.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if @custom.ads_txt}}
{{{@custom.ads_txt}}}
{{else}}
# ads.txt — no authorised digital sellers. This publication currently sells
# no programmatic ad inventory. (IAB Tech Lab ads.txt specification.)
{{/if}}
